      "problem": "Simplify $-6x^2\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2\\right).$",
      "worked_solution": "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) & = \\\\ -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order.",
